              "id": "5f721d09-57eb-3679-b75c-1ab6eae82536",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Insecure Cipher Mode Utilized\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or insecure cryptographic algorithms or modes in a C++ program. This can lead to various security issues, such as data exposure, unauthorized access, or data tampering. The vulnerability sink in this case is the Cipher Text Stealing (CTS) mode. While CTS itself is not inherently insecure, its misuse or incorrect implementation can lead to vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use secure and up-to-date cryptographic algorithms and modes. Avoid using deprecated or weak cryptographic algorithms. Always use cryptographic libraries that are widely accepted and have been thoroughly tested by the security community. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout a specific code example, it's hard to provide a precise fix. However, if you're using a library like OpenSSL, you should ensure you're using a secure mode like AES in CBC or GCM mode. Here's a general example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <openssl/aes.h>\n#include <openssl/rand.h>\n\n// ...\n\nunsigned char key[AES_BLOCK_SIZE]; // AES256 key\nunsigned char iv[AES_BLOCK_SIZE]; // IV\n\n// Ensure you securely generate a random key and IV\nif(!RAND_bytes(key, AES_BLOCK_SIZE) || !RAND_bytes(iv, AES_BLOCK_SIZE)) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\nAES_KEY encryptKey;\nif(AES_set_encrypt_key(key, 256, &encryptKey) < 0) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n\n// Your data to encrypt\nunsigned char data[] = \"Sensitive data\";\nunsigned char encryptedData[sizeof(data)];\n\n// Encrypt data\nAES_cbc_encrypt(data, encryptedData, sizeof(data), &encryptKey, iv, AES_ENCRYPT);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the OpenSSL library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## CWE\n\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)"
