            {
              "id": "ef87cbf5-8f93-37c3-b146-23b95288c7b5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which is a standard library function in C++ that converts a string into an integer. The problem with `atoi()` is that it does not perform any error checking. If the input string cannot be converted into an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `atoi()`. Instead, use functions that perform error checking. In C++, you can use `std::stoi()`, which throws an exception if the conversion fails. Always validate and sanitize user input to prevent injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `atoi(buf)` with `std::stoi(buf)`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n\ntry {\n    int number = std::stoi(buf);\n} catch (std::invalid_argument const &e) {\n    // The input was not a valid integer\n} catch (std::out_of_range const &e) {\n    // The integer was too large for an int\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<string>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "67381261-8481-3e86-bb79-2f5da772756d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions in a memory copy operation. The destination and source addresses may overlap, as indicated by the dataflow analysis, which can lead to unpredictable results when using the standard memory copy function. Additionally, the source pointer may be null, which could cause a crash or further undefined behavior. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent these issues, and the function verdict confirms this as a genuine issue. The destination buffer's size is not known, and the number of bytes to copy is not derived from the destination's capacity, increasing the risk of buffer overflows or memory corruption.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, add a runtime check to ensure they do not overlap before calling the memory copy function. If overlap is possible, use a safe memory move function that handles overlapping regions correctly.\n\n```c\nif ((payload_vaddr < (const void*)addr + nbytes) && ((const void*)addr < payload_vaddr + nbytes)) {\n    // Regions overlap, use memmove\n    memmove(payload_vaddr, (const void*)addr, nbytes);\n} else {\n    // No overlap, safe to use memcpy\n    memcpy(payload_vaddr, (const void*)addr, nbytes);\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, replace the memory copy function with a memory move function, which is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely.\n\n```c\nmemmove(payload_vaddr, (const void*)addr, nbytes);\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a1033452-c3ec-3874-aabe-1af6174802f6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in a call to the strncpy function. Overlapping memory regions in such functions can lead to undefined behavior, which is a serious correctness and security risk. The risk is further confirmed by the explicit classification as an undefined behavior risk and a verdict of 'GENUINE'. The analysis also found that the source and destination are not null, so the main concern is the potential for memory overlap. No explicit buffer size or null-termination handling is present, and there are no guards to prevent this issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when using functions like strncpy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add a runtime check before the call to verify that the memory regions are distinct:\n\n```c\nif ((idp->id_usnic_name + sizeof(idp->id_usnic_name) <= ibdev_buf) ||\n    (ibdev_buf + strlen(ibdev_buf) <= idp->id_usnic_name)) {\n    strncpy(idp->id_usnic_name, ibdev_buf, sizeof(idp->id_usnic_name) - 1);\n    idp->id_usnic_name[sizeof(idp->id_usnic_name) - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap error\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the source and destination do not overlap before performing the copy. Always null-terminate the destination buffer after strncpy to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlap between source and destination is possible or cannot be ruled out, use memmove instead of strncpy, as memmove is defined for overlapping memory regions:\n\n```c\nsize_t copy_len = sizeof(idp->id_usnic_name) - 1;\nsize_t src_len = strnlen(ibdev_buf, copy_len);\nmemmove(idp->id_usnic_name, ibdev_buf, src_len);\nidp->id_usnic_name[src_len] = '\\0';\n```\nThis approach safely handles overlapping regions and ensures the destination is null-terminated.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "94124cbf-50b9-3a8f-9db2-33b4774c95d1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. One such function is `fprintf`.\n\nThe `fprintf` function can lead to vulnerabilities if not used correctly. It can cause format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not a constant, or if it's composed dynamically, it can lead to buffer overflows if the output is larger than the buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `fprintf` such as `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. These functions include the size of the buffer as an argument, which can prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `fprintf`, use `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid safe_function(const char* format, ...) {\n    char buffer[1024];\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    vsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);\n    va_end(args);\n    // Now buffer contains the formatted string.\n    // You can write it to file with fwrite or similar\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- stdio.h\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n\n## CWE\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "204db98e-3b1e-3eb8-ab21-c2aacdb9e394",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. One such function is `fprintf`.\n\nThe `fprintf` function can lead to vulnerabilities if not used correctly. It can cause format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not a constant, or if it's composed dynamically, it can lead to buffer overflows if the output is larger than the buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `fprintf` such as `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. These functions include the size of the buffer as an argument, which can prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `fprintf`, use `snprintf` or `vsnprintf`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid safe_function(const char* format, ...) {\n    char buffer[1024];\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    vsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);\n    va_end(args);\n    // Now buffer contains the formatted string.\n    // You can write it to file with fwrite or similar\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- stdio.h\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n\n## CWE\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
